§ `One month from 2025-01-31`

-- § 6804: a month is a calendar month.  § 6800: the first day is excluded.  31 February does not exist and the text says nothing about a last day that does not: refused,
-- under every calendar and both readings.  (daydate's add months would clamp to 2025-02-28; counting on the days would give 2025-03-03; neither is in the text.)
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) `under the calendar` `calendar G` BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) `with a holiday last day excluded once under the calendar` `calendar G` BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) `under the calendar` `calendar C` BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) `with a holiday last day excluded once under the calendar` `calendar C` BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) `under the calendar` `calendar C plus` BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) `with a holiday last day excluded once under the calendar` `calendar C plus` BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
-- The neighbours of that case: from the 28th, 29th and 30th of January 2025 and from the 31st of March, the month is also short or fine as follows.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 28) EQUALS (YMD 2025 2 28)
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 29) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 30) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2024 1 29) EQUALS (YMD 2024 2 29)
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 3 31) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 3 30) EQUALS (YMD 2025 4 30)
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 12 31) EQUALS (YMD 2026 1 31)

§ `Start on 31, start on 29 February, a month lacking the start day's number: the rest`

-- A start on the 31st of a month: to a month that has a 31st the answer is the 31st; to one that does not, refused.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 2 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) EQUALS (YMD 2025 3 31)
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 3 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 12 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 1 31) EQUALS (YMD 2026 1 31)
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 7 31) EQUALS (YMD 2025 8 31)
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 2 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 7 31) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 5 months `counted from` (YMD 2025 7 31) EQUALS (YMD 2025 12 31)
-- A start on 29 February: years by § 6803 (365 days, the pair counted once), months by the calendar.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 years `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2025 2 28)
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 2 years `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2026 2 28)
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 4 years `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2028 2 28)
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 months `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2024 3 29)
#ASSERT REFUSED `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 12 months `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) BECAUSE "the month reached has no day with the start day's number: Gov. Code § 6804 says only that a month is a calendar month and § 6800 excludes the first day and includes the last, so the text does not say what the last day is (daydate's add months would give the last day of the month, Date would roll into the next month, and the text licenses neither)"
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 48 months `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2028 2 29)
-- Hand check: 29 February 2024 is day 60 of 2024, so 366 - 60 = 306 days remain in 2024, and 31 + 28 = 59 more reach 28 February 2025: 365 days, no leap day in between.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 1 years `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2025 2 28)
-- Three years from 29 February 2024: 1095 days on: the 2028 leap day is in the period and the pair counts once, so one calendar day later than 1095 days on.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of a period of` 3 years `counted from` (YMD 2024 2 29) EQUALS (YMD 2027 2 28)

§ `Commencement: the Act silent on commencement, the Act whose day is fixed later, subordinate legislation`


GIVEN session IS A `Session of enactment`
      d IS A DATE
      adjourned IS A MAYBE DATE
      election IS A BOOLEAN
      tax IS A BOOLEAN
      appropriations IS A BOOLEAN
      urgency IS A BOOLEAN
      own IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH AN `Enactment of a statute`
`test enactment` session d adjourned election tax appropriations urgency own MEANS
    `Enactment of a statute` WITH
        `the session` IS session
        `the moment of enactment` IS Moment WITH `the day` IS d, `seconds after midnight` IS 36000
        `the special session adjourned on` IS adjourned
        `calls an election` IS election
        `provides for a tax levy` IS tax
        `provides for appropriations for the usual current expenses of the state` IS appropriations
        `is an urgency statute` IS urgency
        `names its own day of effect` IS own

-- An Act passed or published on 2026-03-10 that says nothing about its commencement, at a regular session: the 90th day after 2026-03-10 is 2026-06-08, so 2027-01-01.
#ASSERT `the day a statute goes into effect` (`test enactment` `a regular session` (YMD 2026 3 10) NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING) EQUALS (YMD 2027 1 1)
#ASSERT REFUSED `the moment a statute goes into effect` (`test enactment` `a regular session` (YMD 2026 3 10) NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING) BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9600(a) names the day a statute goes into effect and no moment of it; § 6806 says only that a day runs from one midnight to the next, and Civ. Code § 2 and Code Civ. Proc. § 2 (noon) speak of those codes alone"
-- The same Act, an urgency statute: that day, at the moment it was enacted.
#ASSERT `the day a statute goes into effect` (`test enactment` `a regular session` (YMD 2026 3 10) NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE NOTHING) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 10)
#ASSERT (`the moment a statute goes into effect` (`test enactment` `a regular session` (YMD 2026 3 10) NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE NOTHING))'s `seconds after midnight` EQUALS 36000
-- An Act whose day is 'to be fixed later', fixed as 2026-06-01: § 9600 has no exception for it, so the day is refused; the helper that takes the Act's own day to govern gives 2026-06-01.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day a statute goes into effect` (`test enactment` `a regular session` (YMD 2026 3 10) NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (JUST (YMD 2026 6 1))) BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9600 does not say what happens when a statute names its own day of effect: the exceptions in (b) are elections, tax levies, appropriations for the usual current expenses of the state and urgency statutes; the text neither lets the statute's own day displace (a) nor forbids it"
#ASSERT `the day a statute goes into effect, taking the day it names itself to govern` (`test enactment` `a regular session` (YMD 2026 3 10) NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE (JUST (YMD 2026 6 1))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 6 1)
-- Subordinate legislation made on 2026-03-10 and silent about its commencement: no deposited section says.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day silent subordinate legislation comes into force, when made on` (YMD 2026 3 10) BECAUSE "no deposited section says when subordinate legislation (a regulation, rule or order) takes effect: Gov. Code § 9600 speaks of statutes and § 9602 of concurrent and joint resolutions"
-- 'Passed or published': the day the statute is enacted is the one supplied.  An Act passed on 2 October 2026 and signed on 4 October 2026 binds from different days by the supplied date (fork F-enactment).
#ASSERT `the day a regular-session statute goes into effect when enacted on` (YMD 2026 10 2) EQUALS (YMD 2027 1 1)
#ASSERT `the day a regular-session statute goes into effect when enacted on` (YMD 2026 10 4) EQUALS (YMD 2028 1 1)
-- The codes themselves: the Civil Code and the Code of Civil Procedure at noon on 1 January 1873; the Government Code's own day is not in the deposit.
#ASSERT (`the moment the Civil Code takes effect`)'s `the day` EQUALS (YMD 1873 1 1)
#ASSERT (`the moment the Code of Civil Procedure takes effect`)'s `seconds after midnight` EQUALS 43200
#ASSERT REFUSED `the moment the Government Code takes effect` BECAUSE "no deposited section gives the day or the moment the Government Code itself took effect: Gov. Code § 4 refers to 'this code takes effect' without saying when"

§ `Deemed service or notice: the deposit has no general rule`

-- (see test-usca-general.l4: the function that answers it is a named refusal.)
